    {
        "content": "<p>Dear All,</p>\n<p>here is a puzzle, in the tradition of the Friday afternoon.</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Tactic</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Conv</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">variable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">h</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∈</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"c1\">-- Which of the two `a` changes to `b`?</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">conv_lhs</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">rw</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">h</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"gr\">sorry</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 576298370,
        "sender_full_name": "Damiano Testa",
        "timestamp": 1772208708
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>With or without <code>set_option backward.isDefEq.respectTransparency false</code>?</p>",
        "id": 576298729,
        "sender_full_name": "Matthew Ballard",
        "timestamp": 1772208813
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>is it</p>\n<div class=\"spoiler-block\"><div class=\"spoiler-header\">\n<p>my answer</p>\n</div><div class=\"spoiler-content\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\n<p>the one in <code>[a]</code> because <code>Membership.mem</code> has its arguments reversed</p>\n</div></div>",
        "id": 576300455,
        "sender_full_name": "Aaron Liu",
        "timestamp": 1772209287
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I just encountered this and got mildly annoyed. Any way to fix this?</p>",
        "id": 576440924,
        "sender_full_name": "Weiyi Wang",
        "timestamp": 1772299728
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><code>calc</code> suffers from the same problem when using <code>∈</code>, making it basically unusable in <code>calc</code> (e.g. when doing transitivity with <code>⊆</code>).</p>",
        "id": 576442501,
        "sender_full_name": "Jovan Gerbscheid",
        "timestamp": 1772301368
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>AFAIR, this was introduced some time ago to resolve the following issue: lemmas/instances about <code>{a // a ∈ s}</code>, where <code>s</code> is a <code>Subgroup</code> or a <code>Submodule</code> were not getting <code>Subgroup</code>/<code>Submodule</code> in their discrimination tree keys.</p>",
        "id": 576449527,
        "sender_full_name": "Yury G. Kudryashov",
        "timestamp": 1772307890
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Should <code>conv</code>/<code>calc</code> etc. specialize its logic around <code>∈</code> to use the correct visual order?</p>",
        "id": 576452590,
        "sender_full_name": "Weiyi Wang",
        "timestamp": 1772311272
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Possibly, we should swap sides back and fix the set like issue in a different way.</p>",
        "id": 576458339,
        "sender_full_name": "Yury G. Kudryashov",
        "timestamp": 1772318311
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>The reason for the current order is that we can make use of eta reduction (i.e. <code>fun x =&gt; f x</code> reducing to <code>f</code>) to get rid of the lambda, and lambdas are essentially not indexed by the discrimination tree. To fix this in another way we'd have to index the bodies of lambdas in the discrimination tree.</p>",
        "id": 576459431,
        "sender_full_name": "Jovan Gerbscheid",
        "timestamp": 1772319381
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Another way is to use a type synonym for coercion of set like to type</p>",
        "id": 576459896,
        "sender_full_name": "Yury G. Kudryashov",
        "timestamp": 1772319871
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>E.g., make <code>Elem</code> semireducible</p>",
        "id": 576460700,
        "sender_full_name": "Yury G. Kudryashov",
        "timestamp": 1772320718
    }
]